<% sweep = Lab::SWEEPS.fetch("world_size") %>
<% sides = sweep.fetch(:param_grid).fetch("world_size").map { |arm| arm.fetch("width") } %>
<% epochs = number_with_delimiter sweep.fetch(:epochs), delimiter: " " %>
<% rate_exponent = -Math.log2(Lab::Schema.run_defaults.fetch("mutation_rate")).round %>

<%# The recorded result of the sweep. Runs are named by arm and seed, never by a database
    id, and the live tables further down re-read the same runs. %>
<% arms = [
     { label: "32²", side: 32, cells: 1_024, transitions: [], floor: "0.945", entropy: "7.85" },
     { label: "64²", side: 64, cells: 4_096, transitions: [], floor: "0.949", entropy: "7.88" },
     { label: "128²", side: 128, cells: 16_384, transitions: [[9, 15_560]], floor: "0.951", entropy: "7.89" },
     { label: "256²", side: 256, cells: 65_536,
       transitions: [[5, 4_730], [1, 15_800], [3, 19_550]], floor: "0.950", entropy: "7.89" }
   ] %>

<h2>Hypothesis</h2>
<p>
  DESIGN §1.3 sweep 2 sets two readings of emergence against each other, and they predict
  opposite things about a bigger world. If a replicator is a lottery win — one cell
  somewhere stumbles into a tape that copies itself, and the rest of the world only has to
  not destroy it — then cells are tickets: the hazard of a first replicator is a rate
  <em>per cell-epoch</em>, so quadrupling the cell count quadruples the expected number of
  events in a fixed budget, and the smallest worlds should mostly never transition inside
  it. If instead emergence is a property of a world rather than of its cells — a global
  state the soup has to reach — then the hazard is a rate per <em>run</em>-epoch, every arm
  transitions about as often as every other, and cell count buys nothing.
</p>
<p>
  The two readings differ in what a world is for. Under the lottery reading the world is a
  parallel search and its size is the search budget; under the per-world reading the size is
  a detail of the simulation and the clock runs at the same speed in all of them.
</p>

<h2>Method</h2>
<p>
  Square tori of 64-byte tapes at
  <span class="mono"><%= sides.map { |side| "#{side}×#{side}" }.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %></span>
  — width and height travel together, so the arms are
  <%= sides.map { |side| number_with_delimiter(side * side) }.to_sentence(last_word_connector: " and ") %>
  cells —
  with <%= sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %> seeds each and <%= epochs %> epochs per run. Every
  other parameter is at its default, which means a mutation rate of
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-<%= rate_exponent %></sup></span> and a neighbourhood radius of
  1. The dependent variable is the transition epoch of DESIGN §1.2, the first sampled epoch
  at which <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> falls below 0.6 and stays below it for
  three more samples; a run that never crosses is censored, not zero.
</p>

<h2>Result</h2>
<p>
  Partial. Four of the 40 runs transitioned, and the counts rise with cell count. Two of the
  four — both in the largest arm — are still resuming from their snapshots after a deploy
  interrupted them, so they have not reported their own last epochs yet; their transitions
  are recorded and both fell well before the epoch each run has already reached, so neither
  the counts below nor the hazard depends on their finishing. The table is the recorded
  per-arm result; the phase diagram, the survival curves and the per-run rows below read the
  same runs live.
</p>

<div class="table-scroll">
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>World</th>
        <th class="numeric">Cells</th>
        <th class="numeric">Transitioned</th>
        <th>Transition epochs</th>
        <th class="numeric">Median final <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span></th>
        <th class="numeric">Median final <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span></th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% arms.each do |arm| %>
        <tr>
          <td class="mono"><%= arm.fetch(:label) %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= number_with_delimiter arm.fetch(:cells), delimiter: "\u{00A0}" %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:transitions).size %> of <%= sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %></td>
          <td class="mono">
            <% if arm.fetch(:transitions).empty? %>
              —
            <% else %>
              <%= safe_join(arm.fetch(:transitions).sort_by(&:last).map { |seed, epoch|
                    safe_join([number_with_delimiter(epoch, delimiter: " "), " (",
                               findings_run_link(experiment_slug: "world-size", seed: seed,
                                                 params: { "width" => arm.fetch(:side),
                                                           "height" => arm.fetch(:side) }),
                               ")"])
                  }, ", ") %>
            <% end %>
          </td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:floor) %></td>
          <td class="numeric mono"><%= arm.fetch(:entropy) %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p class="text-sm text-muted">
  The two medians are taken over the runs of each arm that did <em>not</em> transition — the
  soup the arm sits in when nothing emerges — at the last sampled epoch.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Emergence gets more likely as the world gets bigger.</strong> Within the same
  <%= epochs %>-epoch budget the counts run 0, 0, 1 and 3 of
  <%= sweep.fetch(:seeds).size %> from the smallest arm to the largest. The two observables
  agree in the largest arm and only there: all three of its flagged runs also carry a
  <span class="mono">replicator_count</span> census of at least one replicating cell, the
  earliest of them sitting at several hundred replicating cells at its latest sample. Nothing
  below 256² has a census above zero at any sample.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The per-cell reading is the one that fits, and here is the test.</strong> If the
  hazard were a constant per cell-epoch, the expected number of events in an arm would scale
  with its cells multiplied by its epochs at risk — so, scaled from the single event at 128²,
  the arms should show about 0.06, 0.26, 1 and 3.7 events. They show 0, 0, 1 and 3. If
  instead the hazard were constant per run-epoch, the four events would fall roughly evenly
  across the arms' observed lifetimes and about 2 of them would land in the two smallest
  arms, where none did — which happens by chance about one time in eight, so the per-world
  reading is disfavoured and not excluded. Pooled over all four arms, the hazard is
  <span class="mono">2.5 × 10<sup>-10</sup></span> per cell-epoch, exact Poisson 95%
  interval <span class="mono">0.7</span> to <span class="mono">6.4 × 10<sup>-10</sup></span>,
  on four events and <span class="mono">1.6 × 10<sup>10</sup></span> cell-epochs at risk; the
  two arms that have events sit at <span class="mono">3.1 × 10<sup>-10</sup></span> (128²)
  and <span class="mono">2.5 × 10<sup>-10</sup></span> (256²), each inside the other's
  interval. The sweep's own hazard table says the same thing per <em>run</em>-epoch, with
  every run censored where it was last watched: 0 events in 200 000 run-epochs in each of
  the two smallest arms, and intervals more than 200-fold wide wherever there is one event.
  Four events cannot measure an exponent. What they support is the sign — more cells, more emergence — not the claim
  that it is exactly linear in cell count.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>The earliest transition of the whole programme is in the largest world.</strong>
  <%= findings_run_link(experiment_slug: "world-size", seed: 5,
                        params: { "width" => 256, "height" => 256 },
                        label: "The 256² arm's fifth seed") %>
  crossed at epoch 4 730, ahead of the
  <%= link_to "mutation-rate", finding_path("mutation-rate-window") %> sweep's 5 030 and the
  <%= link_to "radius", finding_path("radius-locality") %> sweep's 5 910. That is one run,
  and it is not evidence that a bigger world runs faster per run beyond what the hazard
  already implies: a bigger world draws more events per epoch, so its first event tends to
  be earlier, and with one, one and three events the arms' medians — 15 560 against 15 800 —
  are not a time to emergence.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Below the largest arm, a collapse that is not a replicator.</strong> The one
  flagged run of the 128² arm collapses <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span> from the
  random-soup value to near 7.1, drives <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> to 0.071 and
  lifts <span class="mono">copy_rate</span> off zero — with a census of zero at every sample.
  It is the disagreement class the mutation-rate and radius sweeps also report: a low-entropy
  attractor the substrate can fall into that is not a replicator by the test DESIGN §1.2
  locks. Counted on the census rather than the detector the arms read 0, 0, 0 and 3, which
  is the same direction, steeper.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>Size does not change the pre-emergence soup.</strong> Among the runs that never
  transitioned, the final <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span> sits between 0.93 and 0.96
  in every arm, and the per-arm medians span 0.945 to 0.951 — less than the 0.937 to 0.956
  the 32² arm's own ten runs span between them; <span class="mono">entropy_bits</span> ends
  within a few hundredths of 7.87 everywhere. A 1 024-cell soup and a 65 536-cell soup are
  the same soup. That is worth stating because the radius sweep found the opposite for its
  parameter — there the censored runs' floor moved monotonically from 0.951 to 0.859 as a
  cell's reach widened — so cell count acts on how often something happens, not on what the
  world looks like while nothing does.
</p>

<p>
  <strong>A determinism cross-check, for free.</strong> Every parameter of the 128² arm is a
  default, so its ten runs repeat the mutation-rate sweep's
  <span class="mono">2<sup>-<%= rate_exponent %></sup></span> arm and the radius sweep's
  <span class="mono">radius = 1</span> arm. All three report the same single transition — the
  ninth seed at epoch 15 560 — and the same final <span class="mono">compress_ratio</span>,
  digit for digit, across three experiments run weeks apart. That is the guarantee DESIGN
  §1.1 makes, a run as a function of <span class="mono">(params, seed)</span>, checked rather
  than asserted. It is also a caveat: that arm is not an independent sample, so this sweep
  holds 30 runs of new evidence and 10 of a repeat, and its single 128² event is the same
  event the mutation-rate finding already counted.
</p>

<p>
  Two things keep this partial rather than published. The <%= epochs %>-epoch budget is short
  next to the epochs at which these transitions landed — three of the four fell after 15 000
  — so every arm is heavily censored and the small arms may be slow rather than silent, which
  is exactly the reading a per-cell hazard predicts and cannot be told apart from it on four
  events. And a sweep of ours is only readable as a negative once the
  <%= link_to "BFF positive control", finding_path("bff-control") %> resolves its own census;
  until then an arm sitting at the censoring line says as much about the instrument as about
  the world.
</p>

<h2>What next</h2>
<p>
  The direct extension is already running. The
  <%= link_to "BFF positive control", experiment_path("bff-control") %> is a well-mixed soup
  of 2<sup>17</sup> tapes — twice the largest arm here — and its three mutated seeds have all
  flagged, at 5 400, 9 750 and 14 250 epochs, which is the direction the per-cell reading
  predicts. It is not a fifth arm of this sweep: it drops the neighbourhood as well as
  raising the cell count, and its runs are unfinished, so it extends the programme rather
  than the curve. The lens for the scaling question is the survival and hazard section on the
  <%= link_to "sweep's page", experiment_path("world-size") %>, which spends a censored run's
  whole observed lifetime instead of discarding it; the way to narrow those intervals is more
  seeds or longer runs at the sizes that already produce events, not more sizes.
</p>
